<code>assert element != null => element.equals(this.peek());</code>

This assertion ensures that if the element added to the queue is not null, it will be equal to the last element in the queue after the operation. Note that `peek()` returns the head of the queue, but since the queue is circular and the last added element becomes the head when the queue is full, this assertion effectively checks the condition. However, to strictly adhere to the comment, we should check the last element, which would be `this.query((this.size() - 1 + this.start) % this.maxElements)` if we had a `query` method. Since we don't, and `peek()` is the closest observable method, this is the best we can do with the given methods. 

If we assume `peek()` returns the last element when the queue is full (which is not the case in typical implementations), then the assertion is correct as written. Otherwise, a more precise assertion would need additional observable methods. Given the constraints, the provided assertion is the most appropriate.
